---
layout: article
title: Connect to an environment
description: Point Appwrite SDKs, the REST API, the CLI, and the MCP server at a project environment with the project ID and the environment ID.
---

An environment has no project ID of its own. Apps reach it with the **same endpoint and project ID as production**, plus the **environment ID** you picked when you created it, such as `staging`. Leave the environment ID out and the same code acts on production.

The **Get started** page of an environment lists all three values to copy.

{% only_dark %}
![Connect to this environment card with the endpoint, project ID, and environment ID](/images/docs/environments/dark/connect.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Connect to this environment card with the endpoint, project ID, and environment ID](/images/docs/environments/connect.avif)
{% /only_light %}

# SDKs {% #sdks %}

Add `setEnvironment` next to `setProject` when you create the client:

{% multicode %}
```client-web
import { Client } from "appwrite";

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setEnvironment('staging');
```
```client-flutter
import 'package:appwrite/appwrite.dart';

final client = Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setEnvironment('staging');
```
```server-nodejs
const sdk = require('node-appwrite');

const client = new sdk.Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setEnvironment('staging')
    .setKey('<API_KEY>');
```
```server-python
from appwrite.client import Client

client = Client()
client.set_endpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
client.set_project('<PROJECT_ID>')
client.set_environment('staging')
client.set_key('<API_KEY>')
```
```server-php
<?php

use Appwrite\Client;

$client = (new Client())
    ->setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    ->setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    ->setEnvironment('staging')
    ->setKey('<API_KEY>');
```
{% /multicode %}

The [SDKs page](/docs/sdks#environments) shows the same setup for every client and server SDK.

Read the environment ID from configuration rather than hard-coding it, so one build of your app can run against production or any environment:

```client-web
const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint(import.meta.env.VITE_APPWRITE_ENDPOINT)
    .setProject(import.meta.env.VITE_APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID);

if (import.meta.env.VITE_APPWRITE_ENVIRONMENT_ID) {
    client.setEnvironment(import.meta.env.VITE_APPWRITE_ENVIRONMENT_ID);
}
```

## Sessions and API keys {% #sessions-and-keys %}

An environment has its own users and its own API keys. Sign in with an account that exists in the environment, and create server API keys under **API Keys** while the environment is open in the Console. A production key or session can't act in an environment.

Each environment keeps its own session cookie, so a browser can be signed in to production and to an environment at the same time without the two sessions mixing.

## Realtime and file URLs {% #realtime-and-urls %}

Realtime subscriptions follow the client, so a client with `setEnvironment` receives the environment's events. URLs the SDK builds for opening without headers, such as file previews, file downloads, and avatars, carry the environment as the `environment` query parameter.

# REST API {% #rest %}

Send the environment ID in the `X-Appwrite-Environment` header, next to the project's ID in `X-Appwrite-Project`:

```bash
curl https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/tablesdb \
    -H "X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>" \
    -H "X-Appwrite-Environment: staging" \
    -H "X-Appwrite-Key: <STAGING_API_KEY>"
```

Where a request can't carry headers, such as a link in an `<img>` tag, pass `environment=staging` as a query parameter next to `project`. See the [REST API headers](/docs/apis/rest#headers).

# CLI {% #cli %}

The [Appwrite CLI](/docs/tooling/command-line/installation#project-environments) stores the environment in `appwrite.config.json` as `environmentId`, next to `projectId`. When you link a project that has environments, `appwrite init project` asks which one to use. Pass `--environment-id` to choose without the prompt:

```sh
appwrite init project --project-id "<PROJECT_ID>" --environment-id staging
```

In CI, set `APPWRITE_ENVIRONMENT_ID` instead. [Development workflow](/docs/advanced/environments/workflow) shows how to deploy the same config to each environment.

{% info title="Project settings stay with the project" %}
`appwrite push settings` and `appwrite pull settings` work on the project itself, so they stop with an error while an environment is set. `appwrite push all` and `appwrite pull all` skip settings in an environment and push or pull everything else.
{% /info %}

# MCP server {% #mcp %}

The [Appwrite MCP server](/docs/tooling/ai/mcp-servers#environments) acts in an environment when the model passes `environment_id` with `project_id`. When an agent asks for a project's context, the server lists the project's environments, so you can name one in a prompt:

```
Add a status column to the orders table in the staging environment of my storefront project
```

Pointing an agent at a development environment gives it a complete backend to change, with its own data and users, away from production's.
